
  - [1mIn-app only[0m — a banner, a badge on the task, an "Overdue (3)" count in the header. Fires whenever the app is open.
    No permission prompt, no Service Worker, no platform quirks, fully testable. Costs: silent when the tab is closed,
    which is most of the time.
  - [1mWeb Notifications API (OS toast, tab open)[0m — same timing as above, but surfaces as a real desktop notification so
    you see it in another window. Costs a permission prompt, and browsers punish sites that ask badly. Still [1mcompletely
[0m    [1msilent when the tab is closed[0m — this is the common misunderstanding, and it's worth being blunt about: it does not
    buy background delivery.
  - [1mService Worker + Periodic Background Sync[0m — the only browser-only route to firing with the tab closed. Costs:
    Chromium-only, requires the app be installed as a PWA, the browser decides the interval (it can be hours, or never),
    and it silently doesn't run on iOS at all. Unreliable in a way that makes "you'll be told when it's overdue" a
    promise the app can't keep.
  - [1mActually reliable background delivery[0m — needs a server with Web Push (VAPID) or email/SMS. That's the multi-user
    infrastructure we ruled out, reintroduced for one feature.

  [1mMy recommendation: in-app only, with the OS toast as a small additive layer if you want it.[0m It's honest about what a
  serverless app can do, it's the part that works on every browser including iOS, and nothing about it blocks adding
  real push later if you ever get a backend. Periodic Background Sync is the trap here — it looks like it solves the
  problem and then quietly doesn't, on exactly the device you most want the reminder on.
